# Local dev pulls these from Vercel: `vercel env pull`
#
# Every domain this deployment answers on or reaches out to is set here, and
# nowhere else. `config/domains.json` holds the one committed fallback (the
# site's own origin) and is the only place in the repo a domain is spelled out.
# Run `npm run domains` to see what the current environment resolves to, which
# variable each value came from, and what a move costs — `config/domains.md` is
# the long version.
# Convex — written by `npx convex dev`
CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_SITE_URL=

# Clerk — written by `clerk init` / `clerk env pull`
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL=/auth/sign-in
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL=/auth/sign-up
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL=/dashboard
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL=/dashboard

# The origin this deployment answers on. Invitations bake it into the link they
# email out, so it has to name the environment doing the sending — see
# src/lib/site-url.ts. Set on Vercel for Development (http://localhost:3000) and
# Production; deliberately unset for Preview, where VERCEL_URL names the
# deployment and a stored value could only ever be wrong.
#
# This is also the root-domain move in one variable: brand.url, the sitemap,
# robots.txt, metadataBase, the JSON-LD and every role address below all derive
# from it. What it cannot move is what lives outside this repo — the Vercel
# production domain, the Clerk instance binding, and CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN on
# the Convex deployment. `npm run domains` lists those in order.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# The bare domain the role addresses sit on — help@, privacy@, legal@, and the
# rest. Normally left UNSET: it derives from NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL, which is
# what keeps the addresses in the privacy policy from going stale the moment
# the site moves. Set it only for a cutover where the site has moved and mail
# has not, and unset it again when mail follows.
#
# The derivation skips deployment hostnames — localhost and *.vercel.app — and
# uses config/domains.json instead, so a preview never renders a contact
# address nobody could write to.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND_DOMAIN=

# Activities run on one origin now. They are framed at /learn/<slug> on the
# app's own host, gated by the session like any other signed-in page — see
# src/lib/learn.ts. The bundle each one loads is a cross-origin static file on
# the asset origin below, which is the boundary that actually contains the
# third-party activity code, so there is no separate player host to configure
# and no grant secret to sign. (There used to be both; the removal is why a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PLAYER_ORIGIN or PLAYER_TOKEN_SECRET in an old .env.local is now
# inert and can be dropped.)

# Set on Vercel (Production, Sensitive) only — not needed locally:
#   CONVEX_DEPLOY_KEY  lets the Vercel build run `npx convex deploy`
#   npx convex deployment token create vercel-production --prod

# Set on the Convex deployment, NOT in this file (repeat with --prod for the
# production deployment — the Clerk production instance has its own issuer
# domain and its own webhook signing secret):
#   npx convex env set CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN https://<your-app>.clerk.accounts.dev
#   npx convex env set CLERK_WEBHOOK_SECRET whsec_...

# Where hosted game bundles are served from — the public URL of the R2 bucket,
# which is a custom domain like https://assets.example.org (r2.dev works for a
# smoke test but is rate-limited and not for production traffic).
#
# Moving this domain is one variable and a redeploy. The bucket keeps its
# contents: bundle HTML references its own files relatively — scripts/
# migrate-to-r2.mjs rewrites the one absolute URL upstream ships — so nothing
# in there ever learns its own hostname and nothing is re-uploaded. Point the
# new name at the same bucket in Cloudflare, change this, deploy.
#
# Unprefixed on purpose. src/lib/assets.ts has exactly one importer and it is a
# server component, so the origin has no business in the client bundle and an
# unprefixed variable is read at runtime rather than inlined into every build.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_ORIGIN below is still honoured — it is what `vercel env
# pull` has already written — but ASSET_ORIGIN wins and is the one to set.
ASSET_ORIGIN=

#
# There are ~18,000 bundle files and 5.4 GB of them. They are deliberately not
# in this repo: that is past Vercel's 15,000-file deployment limit, and serving
# them from Vercel would bill every byte as Fast Data Transfer. R2 has no
# egress charge. See src/lib/assets.ts, and scripts/migrate-to-r2.mjs for how
# the bucket gets filled.
#
# Leave UNSET and hosted games simply do not appear — the authored React games
# keep working, which is what makes a bare `next dev` useful with no bucket.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ASSET_ORIGIN=

# NOT set here and never in .env.local: the R2 write credentials. This file is
# pulled from Vercel and read by the app, which only ever reads the bucket over
# plain HTTP and has no business holding keys that can write to it. Export them
# into your shell for the one command that needs them:
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID  R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID  R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  R2_BUCKET
#   node scripts/migrate-to-r2.mjs
